Environment Variables (.env) in Playwright
What are Environment Variables?

Environment Variables are used to store configuration values separately from the test code.

These values can include:

Base URLs
Usernames
Passwords
API Keys
Tokens
Environment-specific settings

Instead of hardcoding values inside the code, we store them in a .env file.

Why are Environment Variables Important?
Without .env
TypeScript
const username = "admin";
const password = "password123";
 
await request.post(
"https://restful-booker.herokuapp.com/auth"
);

Problems:

Sensitive data exposed in code
Difficult to change environments
Credentials may get pushed to GitHub
Poor maintainability

❌ Not recommended

With .env
.env File
Plain Text
BASE_URL=https://restful-booker.herokuapp.com
 
USERNAME=admin
 
PASSWORD=password123

Now configuration is separated from code.

✅ Better Security

✅ Easy Maintenance

✅ Environment Flexibility

Install dotenv
Shell
npm install dotenv
Load Environment Variables
playwright.config.ts
TypeScript
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';
import dotenv from 'dotenv';
 
dotenv.config();
 
export default defineConfig({
use: {
baseURL: process.env.BASE_URL
}
});
What does this do?
TypeScript
dotenv.config();

Loads values from .env into:

TypeScript
process.env
Access Variables

Example:

TypeScript
const username = process.env.USERNAME;
const password = process.env.PASSWORD;
API Example
TypeScript
const auth = await request.post('/auth', {
data: {
username: process.env.USERNAME,
password: process.env.PASSWORD
}
});

Instead of:

TypeScript
username: "admin"
password: "password123"
